message: "If you use this project, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Open TechEd: Revival of Legacy Teaching Instruments for Modern Education"
abstract: >-
  Legacy scientific instruments are often discarded not because their hardware
  has failed, but because the proprietary software that controls them is no
  longer supported on modern operating systems. This project pilots the revival
  of one such instrument, the Thermo Fisher NanoDrop 1000 microvolume
  spectrophotometer, by reverse-engineering its undocumented USB communication
  protocol and writing open-source Python software to control it. The work
  includes a pure-Python driver, integration into PyLabRobot, validation
  against a literature xenon lamp spectrum and a paracetamol absorbance
  measurement, and a demonstration with raw data and plots.
